{
  "concept": "A maximum matching in a bipartite graph is exactly a maximum flow in the unit-capacity network obtained by adding a source into L and a sink out of R, so each matched pair corresponds to one vertex-disjoint unit-flow path from s to t.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a): bipartite graph, left column L of 5 gray circles, right column R of 4 gray circles, thin black crossing edges only between columns",
    "Panel (a) highlight: 2 edges drawn with a thick gray band behind the black line (a matching of cardinality 2, no shared endpoints)",
    "Panel (b): the same bipartite graph, identical node positions and identical edge set",
    "Panel (b) highlight: 3 edges with the thick gray band (a maximum matching of cardinality 3, still vertex-disjoint)",
    "Panel (c): flow network — source circle labeled italic s placed left of the L column, sink circle labeled italic t placed right of the R column",
    "Panel (c) edges: arrows s→each of the 5 L vertices, every original L–R edge redrawn as a rightward arrow, arrows from each of the 4 R vertices → t",
    "Panel (c) highlight: three vertex-disjoint s→L→R→t routes traced with the thick gray band, matching the three shaded edges of panel (b)",
    "Column labels: italic L beneath the left column, italic R beneath the right column (repeated in each panel)",
    "Panel captions: (a), (b), (c) centered below each panel",
    "Legend / annotation: 'gray band = matching edge / unit of flow', 'every edge capacity = 1'",
    "Counter annotation: current matching size |M| and current flow value |f| displayed above the active panel"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "panelView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Panel",
      "options": ["(a) matching of size 2", "(b) maximum matching of size 3", "(c) flow network"],
      "default": "(a) matching of size 2",
      "teaches": "The same graph supports matchings of different sizes, and the directed source/sink version of it is the same picture with capacities added — so matching cardinality and flow value are two readings of one structure."
    },
    {
      "id": "selectPath",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking one shaded s→t route in panel (c) lights it end to end and simultaneously highlights its middle L–R edge in panel (b), showing that one unit of flow is exactly one matched pair."
    },
    {
      "id": "augmentStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Augment matching",
      "states": ["empty", "match 1", "match 2", "match 3 (maximum)"],
      "default": "empty",
      "teaches": "Each press pushes one more unit along an augmenting route, adding or rerouting a shaded edge; the matching grows by exactly one and never breaks the vertex-disjointness condition."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A matching, but not a big one",
      "narration": "Panel (a) shows two shaded edges. No gray edge touches the same circle twice — that is what makes it a matching — but three circles on the left and two on the right are still unmatched.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(a) matching of size 2", "augmentStep": "match 2" }
    },
    {
      "title": "One more disjoint edge",
      "narration": "Panel (b) shades three edges instead of two, covering one extra vertex on each side. This is a maximum matching for this graph: no fourth edge can be added without reusing an endpoint.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(b) maximum matching of size 3", "augmentStep": "match 3 (maximum)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn the graph into a flow network",
      "narration": "Panel (c) adds a source s feeding every L vertex and a sink t fed by every R vertex, and orients all edges left to right. Every edge has capacity 1, so no vertex can carry more than one unit.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(c) flow network", "augmentStep": "empty" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Three units of flow = three matched pairs",
      "narration": "Push flow one route at a time: each shaded s→L→R→t path is vertex-disjoint from the others, and its middle arrow is precisely one of the shaded matching edges of panel (b). Maximum flow value 3 equals maximum matching size 3.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(c) flow network", "augmentStep": "match 3 (maximum)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Unit capacities force every s→t path to use a distinct L vertex and a distinct R vertex, so a maximum integral flow is nothing other than a maximum set of non-overlapping edges — a maximum matching.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three panels side by side on white. Panels (a) and (b) are identical bipartite drawings: a left column of five light-gray circles with dark outlines (label italic L below) and a right column of four such circles (label italic R below), offset vertically so edges cross; edges are thin black straight lines going only between columns. Panel (a) shades two of these edges with a wide light-gray band drawn behind the black line; panel (b) shades three, including one edge from the bottom-left circle. Panel (c) is wider: same two columns plus a small circle labeled italic s to the left and one labeled italic t to the right, all edges now arrowheaded and pointing rightward (s→L, L→R, R→t). Three s→t routes in panel (c) carry the same gray band and must use three distinct L circles and three distinct R circles, mirroring panel (b)'s shaded edges exactly. Captions (a), (b), (c) centered below each panel in serif type. Highlight color for interactive selection should be a saturated accent (e.g. blue or orange) layered over the gray band so the selected route stands out from the other shaded ones."
}

